t  Stanford and unique programs this school  has in the space hmm sure so we have a  one of the biggest resources here on  campus is the Center for social  innovation and they are sort of a  conduit or a plethora of resources for  you in career preparation and as well as  plotting your academic journey so when  you come to school they sort of talk to  you about your your two-year roadmap and  beyond that what does it look like once  you leave the GSB and they bucket you  into one of I think four categories so  you might be a career switch or you  might be an industry vet or you might  just be an explorer it's just kind of  interested in the field  um so based on that that kind of help  give you guidance on what to do I think  one of the other great resources that we  have available is you can actually get a  certificate here a certificate in Social  Innovation and Public Management and we  are a generalized and BA program we  don't do specific tracks that's one of  our only certificate programs and so I  think that's been a really great way to  sort of add a credential to my MBA  showing that this is an area of focus  um and then I joined a lot of sort of  social impact type opportunities in  terms of clubs so we have a club for a  sustainable business we have a club for  social innovation  I joined our impact fund and sourced  impact or social impact startups that we  would invest in and and then I am the  CEO of the challenge for charity which  is the largest and being a nonprofit in  the country and so they were things that  were important to me and I found ways to  work them into my not only my coursework  but my extracurriculars and just other  opportunities yeah my internship GSB  focuses a lot on shaping leaders what  kind of leaders and what are the  leadership values that form the ethos of  the GSB do you want to start sure I'll  start um I think a big thing that I've  learned during my time here is that  there's not one kind of leader that can  be effective there are so many people  who are extremely different than me here  and they're just as effective as me if  not more and a lot of different  situations and I think a big strengths  of the GSB is that they help you narrow  in on what your brand is and what  situation and what situation is this  brand effective and in what situation  could you tweak it in a way that still  feels at an authentic to you and I think  in terms of what they look for passion  is certainly something that resonated a  lot with me when I was visiting the  school when I was talking to students I  think it's not  incidents that the question in the  essays what matters most to you and why  they really want to get at what you're  passionate about what drives you what  gets you out of bed in the morning I  agree with all of that and I think if if  you were to ask people just out in the  general public like what is the brand of  GSB leadership and they would probably  talk to you about how we're in touch  with our feelings and our emotions and I  would say that's definitely been a big  focus intentionally of my first year and  a half um has really been doing this  self-reflection and identifying what I  want to get out of being a leader or  being in business and what it is that  drives me what it is that I'm passionate  about and how I want to show up to other  people and I think one of the most  valuable pieces of the GSB is that  there's just this assumption here that  everyone has the best intent possible  and there's this culture of feedback and  it's really a gift to other people to  let them know how you perceive them and  how they're showing up to you because  that's not always information that you  get in the real world and so what you  may intend to do based on how you're  speaking or what you're doing may not be  how other people perceive it and so  that's been a really valuable part of  this experience to me is understanding  how my intent differs from the impact  that I have on others and and so I think  that that is a self aware leader I guess  is where I think the GSB tries to push  us yeah definitely agree with that  next question what was your most  memorable experience at the GSB I want  to start with that one  I'm sure wow there's so many um well my  first challenge for charity weekend  which was last year was a really great  culminating experience of a lot of hard  work throughout the year and then also  an opportunity to host other business  schools here which helped us or helped  me see GSB through their eyes which is  kind of fun um I'm trying to think that  I also loved our Vegas foam so our foam  is just a weekly social event that we  have on Tuesday nights and and it's just  like a kind of a happy hour type  experience and  so you're we all dress up in 70s clothes  and we take a cheap Southwest flight to  Vegas for the night and then we come  back and that's not normally something I  think I would participate in at this  stage in my life but it is really fun to  do with 200 of your closest friends and  so that was a very memorable experience  and and then otherwise I think just the  friendships that I've made and the trips  we've taken together and that's been a  big part yeah yeah I think for me also a  lot of these travel opportunities have  been really memorable for me I actually  met Heather before we even got to campus  on a trip to Singapore Malaysia that was  organized in the context of our program  and so we spent two weeks in those two  countries learning about their approach  to artificial intelligence and big data  and it was like such a great opportunity  for me to meet some of my classmates  from different backgrounds to get to  know them in a bit of a smaller  environment and to really feel like I  had a lot of friendly faces on campus  when I already got there and on top of  that I got to learn about a new industry  you learn about two new countries  experience their culture which was  really exceptional this is a fun  question so can I add two more yeah  quickly so when so Karl and I are very  very good friends I actually had the  honour of introducing Karl for like a  big event that he did on campus he gave  a big speech and so that was a really  memorable  I think opportunity for us together um  and then for me um another big piece  because we're arbuckle fellows we had  the opportunity to take the  interpersonal dynamics class last spring  and and that's sort of a small group  type learning focused on yeah  self-awareness sometimes it's called  touchy feely and that course had a huge  impact on me not only in a professional  setting but how I approach my  relationships and friendships and um has  really impacted a lot about how I live  my life yeah those two are also really  really impactful experiences for me  definitely next can you speak more on  the health care focused opportunities  both at the GSB and beyond for example  classes clubs exit opportunities etc I  think I can take that one  I've taken a lot of health care classes  both at the GSB and what we call across  the street so in other Stanford  faculties I've taken one on innovation  healthcare where each class is about a  different segment of the healthcare  industry we had lots of speakers come in  CEOs a pharma company is a biotech  company is of drug discovery medical  device company is to talk to us about  how they perceive the industries what  are the major trends within their  segment and that was super interesting  for me to see healthcare from the sort  of the business perspective rather than  the clinical perspective I'm also taking  a healthcare operations management class  where I've learned how to take large  chunks of data of the hospital patient  transfers and then like see in a  consulting type of project how we could  optimize transfers between the different  departments in terms of the club our  healthcare club is very active they  always organize brown bag lunches with  different speakers from startups and  large companies alike they organize this  week Trek to a bunch of the startups  operating in the mental health space in  Silicon Valley and they're also  organizing a large conference later in  the spring and finally we're asking  about exit opportunities I think there's  people who do all sorts of things within  healthcare whether health care investing  in VC some people will go work at  healthcare startups some people will do  general health care administration some  people like me will work in ask  clinicians with leadership roles within  the healthcare system and so I think the  world is kind of your oyster within the  healthcare industry once you leave leave  here is there anything you wish you had  known or done differently before coming  into the MBA yes I think that the  opportunities that you're presented at  Stanford are numerous there's always  going to be a lot of competing options  for your  time and attention at any given time  there's four brown bag lunches that you  want to go to and they're all great um I  just had it with sending emails with my  co-founder I was like I'm gonna go see  this former founder can you go talk to  the VC people in the other room and so  um it's a matter of making choices about  the opportunities that you take and I  think something that would have helped  me was to do a little bit more  introspection and figure out my focus  and goals before I came here I had this  general idea that I wanted a reason or a  mission to get up in the morning and to  go to work and that was doing good in  the world but I had no idea what that  meant and I think a tangible example of  that for me as I was applying for our  impact fund and we do investment deals  in a variety of areas and financial  technology healthcare education energy  and environment Urban Development kind  of all over the place and they asked us  to choose what we were interested in and  I knew that I was interested in  investing in things that were good for  the world but I had no idea which of  those species I was actually interested  in and that meant that I was kind of all  over the place and I didn't focus and  once I was able to sort of narrow in on  what it was that I truly cared the most  about well I've got a lot easier for me  it was a lot easier to say no to things  that weren't quite relevant I think  there's always this element of oh well  everybody dropped their resume at Google  like I should probably drop my resume at  Google too and maybe that's true but  maybe it's not and so just knowing some  of these things about yourself before  you come here and all of these shiny  objects they're thrown out you know I  think can be a really helpful exercise  yeah I'll go in a slightly different  direction for this one I wish I had  known more about the power of first of  Stanford Network but also of a Stanford  email an example that I've given a few  people recently is that over the past  month I've emailed for hospital CEOs  just to ask them to sit down and chat  with me and all four of them responded  three out of the four responding within  24 hours and and we we set up a little  like small short calls of like half an  hour and they were all like very willing  to tell me about their experiences  how they got to where they got and to  give me advice on how to get where they  are at and so had I known about that  earlier I think I would have taken much  more advantage of that last year even as  soon as I got my Stanford email when I  first got it what has been the greatest  challenge you have faced at Stanford  okay okay I'll take that one um I think  two things first I'm an international  student and so adapting to new culture  and a new country has been more  difficult than I anticipated especially  because I I come from Canada so I  expected the culture to be very similar  but there are some differences in terms  of the politics in terms of the culture  the cultural references and so it was  kind of interesting for me to realize  that I was in a very different  environment sort of midway through my  Fall Quarter and had I braced myself a  bit more for that I think my transition  would have been a bit smoother and the  other thing is time management  like as Heather was mentioning there's  so many opportunities that are thrown at  you here all the time but it's hard  sometimes to decide which one is the  best use of your time  I would definitely echo time management  I think for me the second dimension is  much more personal and that's that my  husband lives in Chicago and so we're in  a long-distance marriage we got married  two months before I moved here to start  my two-year MBA program and and that's  hard we're doing well but of course we  would rather be together um so I think  just figuring out I think a lot of  people struggle with how to balance  their relationships and lives pre GSB  with the fact that this is an  all-consuming experience and that you  really want to be present for and take  advantage of over the two years that  you're here and so finding that balance  for yourself and how to take care of  yourself and your other relationships  outside of the GSB what's the single  most important area Stanford has offered  you to improve your market value so far  my market value yeah that's a very  interesting one I think I have an idea  actually  great I would say it's made me more  aware of my market value anything I  think like as someone who was more an  individual contributor I didn't realize  how valuable the skills that I already  possessed before the GSB were and I  didn't realize that there were so many  different things that I could be doing  within and outside healthcare with what  I've learned both here and before and so  I think Stanford has taught me to think  big and know that if I work hard and if  I am passionate about what I'm doing I  can accomplish a lot of things that I  never even thought were possible in the  past I'm gonna work this in two  directions I think first what I think  outside perception of my market value is  and then like where I actually think my  market value has improved the most from  an internal perspective because I think  those are different things  I'm from the outside perspective  honestly just the signal of a Stanford  MBA credential is what I think has  improved my market value the most like  there are groups that I emailed  beforehand as a consultant not a very  good consulting firm prior to the GSB  when I was kind of thinking about  different career options when I was  debating between that in business school  and I never got an email back and then  suddenly you are a stand for an MBA and  everyone emails you back so just having  the credential is in of itself of market  value and and that feels a little  transactional to say but I think it's  there and and then internally where I  actually think that I have games the  most market value is in the soft skills  absolutely I think that before when I  was in really high pressure consulting  environments it was easy for me to be  impatient or speak sharply in a way that  would could be demotivating to a team  and I've really learned how to adapt  sort of a firm style with being a little  bit more empathetic and when I think  we'll maybe they'll come across in  interviews maybe they'll be valued but  they will certainly be valued when I'm  managing teams or leading teams in the  fact that I feel much more equipped to  develop and grow it  to their utmost potential how  collaborative is the GSB environment do  the second-year students help with  recruiting preparation if the classroom  dynamics friendly yeah classroom  dynamics very friendly  we have a student agreed upon norm here  of grade non-disclosure and which means  that to recruiters you aren't allowed to  share your class rank you're not allowed  to share your GPA and so it's super  collaborative like I think last year  people were building study guides and  sending them out to the entire class  saying hey you know I compiled all the  slides good luck on your finals  so definitely collaborative and helpful  it's not competitive I don't think at  all and then the question do second-year  students help with recruiting  preparation yeah I think so a large  portion of our class is interested in  slightly more entrepreneurial ventures I  think where I see the most help in  career preparation is that if there's  somebody that's in the field that you  want to go into they will if you email  them and they're like hey can we sit  down for 30 minutes for coffee they will  tell you about their summer experience  we do a ton of panels I'm like what did  I do last summer how did I go through  that process what did it look like um  and then there are a few things that are  a little bit more institutionalized and  so um for consulting for example I ran  cases with a lot of people last year to  help prepare them for their case  interviews and you maybe you can speak  more about that from your perspective  yeah I was recruiting for consulting and  obviously we have a lot of classmates  worked in consulting we have consulting  club and so I leaned into both of those  resources pretty heavily to prepare for  my interviews and that was honestly that  made the difference between me getting  the job or not at the end of the day and  so I think it's incredibly collaborative  the other thing I'll say about that is  that we all become kind of known to each  other because the class size is small  enough that you have it at least an idea  of what people are interested in whether  I like a little bit and so whenever I  hear of an opportunity and social impact  or in  for example I'll think of Heather  immediately and if she hears of  something within healthcare like  healthcare systems policy like she'll  think of me  and so opportunities kind of get  redirected a lot like that which is  super helpful for us to achieve her  goals and learn about things that we  wouldn't have heard about otherwise I  think people are super generous with  their networks here I'm and pass along  job opportunities that might be of  interest all the time and if there's  something that you mentioned they'll be  like oh I have a friend that does that  let me introduce you so I think that's  been super helpful yeah definitely how  was your experience with the application  process and what is your piece of advice  to potential applicants great and I  think this will probably have to be our  last question I think so the experience  with the application process  I think Stanford was by far the most  involved application that I completed so  I would say open it up and look through  it entirely well in advance of the  deadline if you if you know the essay  prompts and you think you'll just do  that and fill in your name and address  there's more to it than that  so look in advance um but overall that  application process was great um I think  my advice for you is well first of all  again this is a black box none of us  knows exactly why we were admitted so we  don't say anything on behalf of the  admissions office and and for me I think  it was really important to me that I  show that I was a well-rounded person  who had interests and passions and just  things that I cared about outside of  work and if you have turned your passion  into your work that is great talk about  your work by all means but show all the  facets of your personality yeah I think  the only thing I'd add to that is just  try to be as authentic as you can none  of us know what the admissions office is  looking for so you may as well be  authentic self so that you know that the  person they accepted is the person you  actually are not a person that you've  tried to manufacture for an admissions  committee I think they're very  intentional about creating a community  and you want to be you want to know that  you're the person that they wanted as  part of this community self-reflection  is good  definitely so we're almost out of time  unfortunately but before we go Heather  do you have any final words to share  with the applicants or a piece of advice  mmm this is the greatest two years ever  I'm so just have fun try not to stress  too much um I  the application process seems  never-ending but it's really worth it on  the other side so yeah try to enjoy it  and use it as an opportunity for  self-reflection and then do more of that  over your two years of business school  wherever you go amazing thanks everyone  for joining us and we hope you learned a  lot over the course of this panel  [Music]


